WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.071
[FOREIGN] California.

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:08.230
Hello, and welcome to our webcast,

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:12.370
Germany, Austria, Switzerland, Stanford
Graduate School of Business and beyond.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:16.190
My name is Tim Eisenmann, and I'm a second
year MBA student here at the GSB.

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:20.544
I am very proud to be graduating in
just a couple of weeks, also a bit sad.

00:00:20.544 --> 00:00:23.680
I'm joined by Desiree Peill and
Dominik Facher.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:24.900
Like me, they are from Germany.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.035
They are first year MBA students and
will graduate in 2016.

00:00:28.035 --> 00:00:31.520
We'll be talking about the student
experience here on campus and

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.180
answer as many of your questions as we
can, including those submitted in advance.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.900
We won't be talking about the admissions
process, the application, or

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.010
financial aid.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:44.286
You can find these answers
on the Stanford MBA website.

00:00:44.286 --> 00:00:47.500
To submit a question to us about
what it's like to be a student,

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:50.020
just click the Chat icon
at the top of your screen,

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.370
enter your question in the Chat window,
and click Send to All in your screen.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.500
To kick us off, I'd suggest that maybe
we introduce ourselves a little bit,

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.060
so you know who you'll
be hearing from today.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:03.460
Desiree, would you like to kick us off?

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.460
&gt;&gt; Sure.
My name is Desiree.

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.570
I'm an MBA1 student here at the GSB.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:14.040
Prior to the GSB, I worked in
consulting with a focus on media.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:21.700
I did my undergrad in Switzerland, in
[FOREIGN], and after that worked a lot for

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.300
various media companies as a journalist
and then later on the business side.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:30.580
And I'm very happy to be here at the GSB
and excited to talk to you about it today.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:33.621
&gt;&gt; Thanks.
Yeah, so I'm Dominik,

00:01:33.621 --> 00:01:34.677
originally from Munich.

00:01:34.677 --> 00:01:41.362
I grew up a little bit outside the city,
so all countryside, mountains, outdoors.

00:01:41.362 --> 00:01:44.023
That's what I enjoyed
during the youth time.

00:01:44.023 --> 00:01:48.389
And, yeah, when you're not outside and
you're in the countryside,

00:01:48.389 --> 00:01:52.780
the first thing that comes to mind
when you don't do that is computers.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.740
So, I always was very
attached to technology.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:02.390
I studied Computer Science as a result of
that, studied in Munich and in Zurich.

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:06.812
And after that, we started
a company in the field of, yeah,

00:02:06.812 --> 00:02:12.153
recruiting social networks, so
I worked as a software engineer there.

00:02:12.153 --> 00:02:14.640
And after we finished
up with that company,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.540
I worked in consulting as well,
mainly in the technology space.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:22.760
And, yeah,
now I'm here at the GSB as well.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:23.970
&gt;&gt; As I said already, my name is Tim.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.130
I'm from Frankfurt originally.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:28.090
I used to work in transportation for

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.275
Lufthansa, one of the big
German companies.

00:02:30.275 --> 00:02:33.150
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And in corporate strategy for two and

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.790
a half years.

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:38.600
Then did a lot of transportation
focus here at the GSB.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:43.300
We found a class that was present
at the travel hospitality club and

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:47.580
also did a little bit of work in
the social impact sector in Africa,

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.690
two very different things, and post GSB,

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:54.190
I'll be doing consulting in the outland
transportation sector as well.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.430
Well, it's, maybe,
get a head start and go and

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.300
answer one of the questions
that were submitted in advance.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.565
Why did you guys apply to Stanford?

00:03:04.565 --> 00:03:06.180
[LAUGH] Or business school in general?

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:09.927
What were the driving factors,
and why Stanford in particular?

00:03:09.927 --> 00:03:11.247
Maybe Dominik, do you wanna start?

00:03:11.247 --> 00:03:12.649
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure.
Yeah, I mean, for

00:03:12.649 --> 00:03:15.007
me it's basically the place to be, right?

00:03:15.007 --> 00:03:19.999
So I wanted to be in the Silicon Valley
cuz this place is really where

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:22.430
sort of the future gets built.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:27.060
This is where all the big companies, all
the ideas come together, and Stanford, for

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:30.820
me, was sort of really
central in that ecosystem.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.566
And I think it's a very special place
in that sense that you find in very few

00:03:35.566 --> 00:03:38.205
business schools, if any, actually.

00:03:38.205 --> 00:03:43.220
So that was a key driver for me, and
besides that, I was always sort of

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:48.600
torn to that technology part of it so
that made it sort of an easy choice for

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.830
me to chose Stanford as a business school.

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:56.484
And then you asked sort of why
business school in general, so for

00:03:56.484 --> 00:04:00.951
me it was always I come very much from
the technology and engineering side, and

00:04:00.951 --> 00:04:05.910
then at some point realized that sorta
there's a business side as well to it.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:08.550
I learned a little bit of
that in consulting, but

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:12.330
essentially, I saw the very small
companies when they get started, and

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.200
I saw the really big companies.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.060
It's like the really, really big ones,
but I never saw this place in between.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:23.973
And so for me, it was always how does this
go from really small to scaling it pretty

00:04:23.973 --> 00:04:27.750
big, and part of that is
actually scaling yourself, right?

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:31.598
So how can you scale yourself as in
you have a team of three people, and

00:04:31.598 --> 00:04:34.110
then suddenly you're a thousand people or
even more.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:35.810
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; How can you manage that, and

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:39.050
a lot of that is personal skills,
leadership skills, etc.

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:43.240
And this for me was sort of the main
driver for business school in general.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:44.030
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:45.270
How about you, Desiree?

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.740
&gt;&gt; So why [COUGH] I chose to
apply to business school.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:51.110
I feel like after three years
in consulting I just felt

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.417
like a [FOREIGN] after some time-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:53.417 --> 00:04:55.440
&gt;&gt; Where you just focus on one specific

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.510
topic, get pretty good at it, but it's,
you kind of miss a broader horizon.

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:05.060
And I felt that, in particular,
Stanford would offer that the most to me.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:09.930
In particular, looking at the ecosystem
here, the same you talked about,

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:14.420
it feels like a little bit,
I think like Florence must have felt like

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:18.450
that in the Renaissance where everything
is happening exactly in this little,

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:20.840
tiny piece of land, and
it's just exciting to be here.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:24.630
And then, more specifically for
me, in media and journalism,

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:30.370
it's a hugely disruptive, the industry
is hugely disrupted at the moment,

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:34.130
by a lot of the things which are going on
here, and so I was just very excited to

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:37.400
learn about it and
to know more of what is going on there.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:38.580
&gt;&gt; It does take a little bit of, well,

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.160
convincing and telling other people
though because business school isn't that

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.570
commonly known as
a career path in Germany.

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:47.290
In the US, it's a bit different.

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:48.160
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:50.410
&gt;&gt; But
it does bring us closer to our goals.

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:52.115
Or at least, when I speak for myself.

00:05:52.115 --> 00:05:56.036
Maybe we can add onto what you said
Desiree about being here in an ecosystem

00:05:56.036 --> 00:05:59.780
of entrepreneurship while having
a very strong background in media.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:04.067
How has the GSB helped you get closer
to want you want to do after Stanford?

00:06:04.067 --> 00:06:10.080
&gt;&gt; So kinda coming here, I knew that there
is the Arts Media Entertainment Club,

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.103
for example,
which I am the president of right now.

00:06:14.103 --> 00:06:16.927
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:06:16.927 --> 00:06:18.969
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Which is doing a lot of things

00:06:18.969 --> 00:06:22.775
in this school, kind of bringing
me to executive hear talking,

00:06:22.775 --> 00:06:27.590
bringing companies here to talk about
what's going on in the industry.

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:31.990
And furthermore, there are a bunch
of classes which are kind of hugely

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.680
tailored to exactly that space.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.435
So there's one about leadership and
entertainment.

00:06:36.435 --> 00:06:40.590
I'm taking another one actually not at the
business school but on campus in Stanford,

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:43.800
which is called Entrepreneurial Media.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:49.930
And so we are kind of in that class
already, working in teams and

00:06:49.930 --> 00:06:53.800
working on business ideas and pitching
it to investors this Friday actually and

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:57.290
trying to figure out whether we have
found something, which is interesting.

00:06:57.290 --> 00:07:01.050
And so overall,
I think the GSB is helping me also,

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:03.430
personally, to look at all
these different spaces.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:07.430
It's very easy to actually contact
people and get them to talk to you

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:12.201
about these things, and so it's been kind
of from clubs, from the classes, but

00:07:12.201 --> 00:07:16.366
then also from people who come to
the GSB and talk to me about the topic.

00:07:16.366 --> 00:07:21.446
&gt;&gt; And the @standford.edu email address
opens any door you want to have opened.

00:07:21.446 --> 00:07:22.136
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's true.

00:07:22.136 --> 00:07:26.119
&gt;&gt; And Dominik is casually starting
his own company right on campus.

00:07:26.119 --> 00:07:27.459
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:27.459 --> 00:07:27.965
&gt;&gt; Maybe you can speak

00:07:27.965 --> 00:07:28.536
about that a little.

00:07:28.536 --> 00:07:29.993
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure.
And also,

00:07:29.993 --> 00:07:34.330
I fully agree with what Desiree said.

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:38.930
For me, it was never really
a traditional career path post GSB, so

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:39.860
I never had that in mind.

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:41.605
And there's a lot of
classmates who have that.

00:07:41.605 --> 00:07:45.674
If, for example, if you go into banking,
etc., it's a very clear path.

00:07:45.674 --> 00:07:48.028
And that, for me, was never the case and

00:07:48.028 --> 00:07:52.114
not part of making the decision
towards going to business school, but

00:07:52.114 --> 00:07:55.937
it's all those other elements
that come together, and I think.

00:07:55.937 --> 00:07:58.377
It makes a key difference from the GSP, so

00:07:58.377 --> 00:08:03.002
first of all there's the network you get
right, the access you get to people.

00:08:03.002 --> 00:08:06.403
So for example, I'm in a class
with Peter Levine right now,

00:08:06.403 --> 00:08:10.874
who's the enterprise software partner
from Andreessen Horowitz, which is,

00:08:10.874 --> 00:08:14.427
it's probably the best VC that
you can find out there, right?

00:08:14.427 --> 00:08:19.343
And so you really have access and very
close connections in those people, and

00:08:19.343 --> 00:08:21.091
it's really tremendous.

00:08:21.091 --> 00:08:26.027
And as Tim said, we're sort of starting
our own business right now, and yeah if

00:08:26.027 --> 00:08:30.889
you have access to those people and really
can have a casual chat with them, and

00:08:30.889 --> 00:08:33.559
ask them certain questions that you have.

00:08:33.559 --> 00:08:38.330
It just makes a huge difference right, and
this is sort of the thing then you get

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:41.656
the introduction, and
then you get the next step, and

00:08:41.656 --> 00:08:46.210
most of the stuff that you actually try
to do, you're right at the center of

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:50.300
where your customers are,
where the ideas get sparked.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:53.852
You get all this inspiration
from other schools.

00:08:53.852 --> 00:08:57.628
It's just a five minute walk and
your in Computer Science,

00:08:57.628 --> 00:09:02.410
which is probably also the best
Computer Science Department in the world.

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:06.704
So it's really, it all comes together
in this a weird thing of ecosystem,

00:09:06.704 --> 00:09:11.476
and in the non traditional career path,
I think this is what makes the difference,

00:09:11.476 --> 00:09:16.350
that you have access to all of those
opportunities and just can go into it.

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:20.250
&gt;&gt; One thing I just wanna add is,
that even if you have a very

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:21.670
particular interest, such as
&gt;&gt; Yeah

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:23.570
&gt;&gt; media, I know a couple of people that

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:24.249
are in bio tech.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:29.720
The GSB does offer a lot of classes
already but then, across the street and

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:31.780
basically all the other faculties,

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:34.330
Stanford GSB students can
take classes as well.

00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:38.320
So there are a couple of classmates
that have only taken the minimum of GSB

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:40.920
classes, and taken advantage of
all the across the street classes.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:42.690
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; A lot of people take the base coding

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:47.980
class, just so they know how to
speak with technical co founders.

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:48.740
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Let's maybe

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:53.180
pivot to some of the questions
that were submitted right now.

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:56.980
How has the different education style,

00:09:56.980 --> 00:10:00.990
US versus German speaking
countries played out for you guys?

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:03.313
What were the major differences?

00:10:03.313 --> 00:10:08.712
&gt;&gt; So I think, for me, even though
I studied at a small university,

00:10:08.712 --> 00:10:13.623
classes were still massively huge,
so here we have classes

00:10:13.623 --> 00:10:18.040
from 20 to 70 people and it's-
&gt;&gt; Or even smaller, in lead lab's 12.

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:19.710
&gt;&gt; yeah, 12's true.

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:22.969
And it's, it's fascinating because you-
&gt;&gt; I think it's more than 12-

00:10:22.969 --> 00:10:23.504
&gt;&gt; Four actually.

00:10:23.504 --> 00:10:24.455
&gt;&gt; yeah.

00:10:24.455 --> 00:10:27.271
&gt;&gt; Sorry it was a year ago.

00:10:27.271 --> 00:10:31.175
&gt;&gt; And it's fascinating to have kind of
everybody participating a lot more and

00:10:31.175 --> 00:10:33.620
that's also something
you have to get used to.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:36.680
It's kind of much more, interactive.

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:39.100
People are expecting to speak up.

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:44.123
It's slightly different
than back where I studied.

00:10:44.123 --> 00:10:48.001
But then also, I think we've
been kind of educated to not be

00:10:48.001 --> 00:10:52.823
as controversial when we kind of make
statements, and things like that.

00:10:52.823 --> 00:10:55.331
The discussions don't come up that easily,

00:10:55.331 --> 00:10:59.621
but this is entirely different here,
and people just speak their minds, and

00:10:59.621 --> 00:11:03.595
there's always an interesting
discussion going on in the classroom.

00:11:03.595 --> 00:11:06.203
&gt;&gt; Yeah let's, so
Larry have you talked about how you

00:11:06.203 --> 00:11:08.960
make the most out of what's
happening on campus.

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:11.750
i mean there are so many activities,
what do you prioritize,

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:14.830
was it overwhelming at first, and
how have you managed to do now?

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:16.520
&gt;&gt; Definitely overwhelming.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:18.880
The thing is you can do
whatever you want right.

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:20.430
So if you're curious about things.

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:25.340
There's everything going on, and so
one of the things for me coming in was to

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:29.944
sometimes not do the smart things,
but the sort of more interesting and

00:11:29.944 --> 00:11:34.575
curiosity driven things, and
you can do whatever you want basically.

00:11:34.575 --> 00:11:38.804
So if there is a session on virtual
reality, you just learn more about virtual

00:11:38.804 --> 00:11:43.160
reality, or all those crazy things that
are going on, and sort of the edges that

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:47.671
often times you don't have access to when
you're not in the university context.

00:11:47.671 --> 00:11:53.337
And so, this is sort of the curiosity part
for me, and the other part with resources,

00:11:53.337 --> 00:11:57.746
when you think about actually
a business and starting something,

00:11:57.746 --> 00:12:03.022
there's tremendous resources available
from events to personal coaching's

00:12:03.022 --> 00:12:07.981
to sort of classes where you can actually
learn those things directly, and

00:12:07.981 --> 00:12:10.290
you get mentors that help you etc.

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:15.800
And yeah, so at the end of the day,
I remember when we started you

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:19.590
basically leave your home at eight o'clock
and then you come back at ten o'clock.

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:23.550
You were like busy the whole day,
for me it was more busy then

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:26.407
during when I was working
&gt;&gt; That means Dominique does not

00:12:26.407 --> 00:12:28.961
go out and party, so if he comes home
at ten am [INAUDIBLE] [CROSSTALK].

00:12:28.961 --> 00:12:33.570
&gt;&gt; That’s right
&gt;&gt; He's a boring software engineer.

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:35.510
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but that's about it.

00:12:35.510 --> 00:12:38.585
You go to bed, maybe you party,
maybe you don't.

00:12:38.585 --> 00:12:40.107
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But you go to bed, and

00:12:40.107 --> 00:12:42.778
it's this happy sort of stress, right?

00:12:42.778 --> 00:12:43.337
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:43.337 --> 00:12:47.645
&gt;&gt; You just feel like there was so
much going on, and you feel exhausted, but

00:12:47.645 --> 00:12:52.162
at the same time, it's a good sort of
exhaustion, and yeah and sometimes you

00:12:52.162 --> 00:12:56.679
need to prioritize, but my thing was
always to keep more the filter open, and

00:12:56.679 --> 00:13:00.403
take in as much as possible,
rather than the other way around.

00:13:00.403 --> 00:13:03.515
&gt;&gt; I would totally agree with
that in particular this idea that

00:13:03.515 --> 00:13:06.814
you kind of just get everything,
kind of bombarded at you, but

00:13:06.814 --> 00:13:08.947
on the other hand it's your own stress.

00:13:08.947 --> 00:13:12.925
So priority and consulting is always
like a partner in giving you stress, or

00:13:12.925 --> 00:13:16.341
the client giving you stress,
but right now it's all about you,

00:13:16.341 --> 00:13:20.160
and you can decide everything and
it's incredibly energizing as well.

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:22.050
&gt;&gt; Yeah that's right.

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:27.250
&gt;&gt; One of the question that just came
in asked about the background of your

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:27.860
classmates.

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:31.940
Well a whole bunch of them
are internationals just like us.

00:13:31.940 --> 00:13:34.585
There are of course a lot of classmates.

00:13:34.585 --> 00:13:37.220
&gt;&gt; [COUGH]
&gt;&gt; From South America,

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:39.550
a lot from Asia, Central Europe as well,

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:43.940
I think in my class we had a lot of
Germans, French, Spanish students, but

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:48.915
it varies I mean there is no set amount of
people that come from a specific country.

00:13:48.915 --> 00:13:52.305
It really depends on the application
pool of that specific year.

00:13:52.305 --> 00:13:55.235
But maybe you can talk about some
classmates that have been especially

00:13:55.235 --> 00:13:59.235
inspiring and have had a really
interesting professional background.

00:13:59.235 --> 00:14:00.000
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.269
So I think for me, we have musical stars,

00:14:04.269 --> 00:14:09.650
people who were on American Idol for
example,

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:14.240
and they have the most beautiful voices,
and incredible artistic talent, which is,

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:19.320
we have a big show every year,
where people kind

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:24.300
of produce a one time musical, and it's as
professional as you would see on Broadway.

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:28.520
That was something that I didn't
even expect coming here, and

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:30.795
I was incredibly impressed.

00:14:30.795 --> 00:14:33.120
Then right now I just come out of
a classroom where somebody talked

00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:36.910
about their operations experience
at Formula One, and how he for

00:14:36.910 --> 00:14:42.130
seven years worked as a manager and
general manager in the Middle East for

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:47.258
the Formula One team of thank you.

00:14:47.258 --> 00:14:51.890
[LAUGH] I'm a huge Formula One
fan obviously, but

00:14:51.890 --> 00:14:54.670
it's just entirely different
than a lot of others.

00:14:54.670 --> 00:14:56.890
I think I can think of like more and more.

00:14:56.890 --> 00:14:58.480
Do you have some more?

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:03.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah I mean same, and for
me what was pretty interesting was talking

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:08.630
to people who were in the military before,
which is something that was really not

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:14.180
present whatsoever before in
sort of the European education.

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:18.390
And really hearing how they
experienced their lives before and

00:15:18.390 --> 00:15:21.270
whatever happened, and
you can hardly imagine.

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:23.436
It just gives you a lot of perspective,
right?

00:15:23.436 --> 00:15:27.862
And that happens to all different sorts
of people, like I don't know, we have

00:15:27.862 --> 00:15:33.162
professional poker players, whatever, it's
really interesting, and essentially it's

00:15:33.162 --> 00:15:38.014
different perspectives on things you had
in mind before, and you just challenge it.

00:15:38.014 --> 00:15:41.326
&gt;&gt; And maybe just to give,
so we have each week,

00:15:41.326 --> 00:15:45.424
we have two classmates actually
sharing their story and

00:15:45.424 --> 00:15:49.623
sharing their entire lives
with us in a 30 minute talk.

00:15:49.623 --> 00:15:53.208
That's every Monday for the MBA1s and
every Wednesday for the MBA2s, and

00:15:53.208 --> 00:15:56.680
it's kind of that's the moment where
you get to know them, and you know so

00:15:56.680 --> 00:15:58.472
much more about where they come from,

00:15:58.472 --> 00:16:02.504
the life they've been living before, what
has shaped them, who they are today, and

00:16:02.504 --> 00:16:05.500
it's kind of a very inspiring
to actually get to know people.

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:07.620
&gt;&gt; First, it's a lot of, hey,
what have you been doing?

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:08.302
Where are you from?

00:16:08.302 --> 00:16:10.190
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; What did you do before hand?

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:12.710
And then the GSB I think finds
good ways to connect people-

00:16:12.710 --> 00:16:13.223
&gt;&gt; That's true.

00:16:13.223 --> 00:16:14.760
&gt;&gt; In smaller more meaningful settings.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:16.140
Talk is one of them.

00:16:16.140 --> 00:16:19.000
But there are tons of small group
dinners that are actually supported by

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:20.590
the university financially.

00:16:20.590 --> 00:16:25.010
There are wind groups where women come
together and talk about their experience

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:28.570
in maybe more male-dominated
environments or at business school.

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:32.790
And then, of course, the clubs,
as you've mentioned,

00:16:32.790 --> 00:16:35.580
does host a variety of events with
people that have a similar interest.

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:37.565
So they get to know each
other on a deeper level.

00:16:37.565 --> 00:16:39.140
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; And then all the outside stuff,

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:41.405
like I've actually gotten to
know more people playing tennis-

00:16:41.405 --> 00:16:42.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:44.940
&gt;&gt; Than with any other thing at the GSB.

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:48.743
&gt;&gt; I mean, beyond that, I think It's
not just those kinds of events but

00:16:48.743 --> 00:16:51.837
also that the class size is
smaller in general, right?

00:16:51.837 --> 00:16:55.435
So, yeah, I mean,
we're 400 people roughly?

00:16:55.435 --> 00:16:56.422
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think so.

00:16:56.422 --> 00:17:00.903
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; For me it was something that you can

00:17:00.903 --> 00:17:06.447
actually get to know 400
people quite well, right?

00:17:06.447 --> 00:17:09.453
Versus if it would be 1000 or more people,

00:17:09.453 --> 00:17:13.626
I'm not sure I could get to know each and
everyone in that sense.

00:17:13.626 --> 00:17:17.748
And you always have this moment of where
you actually wanna get to know everyone

00:17:17.748 --> 00:17:19.697
and hear those stories whatsoever.

00:17:19.697 --> 00:17:23.489
And I think 400 people is the size
where you have enough interaction on

00:17:23.489 --> 00:17:24.434
a regular basis.

00:17:24.434 --> 00:17:25.688
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So that works.

00:17:25.688 --> 00:17:29.910
&gt;&gt; So let's dial back for you guys,
probably a year and a half.

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:31.679
You were thinking about
applying to business school.

00:17:31.679 --> 00:17:34.972
How did you know that
that was the right time?

00:17:36.364 --> 00:17:37.688
&gt;&gt; Good question.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:17:37.688 --> 00:17:39.190
&gt;&gt; Was it the right time?

00:17:39.190 --> 00:17:42.450
&gt;&gt; For
me there was no such question actually.

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:46.260
I didn't particularly design this
process of now it's the time to apply.

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:48.254
So it never crossed my mind in that sense.

00:17:48.254 --> 00:17:53.080
I always had this notion in mind
that I need to sort of get back.

00:17:54.090 --> 00:17:55.750
I need the time to sort of
develop those more leadership,

00:17:55.750 --> 00:18:01.010
more long-term oriented skills, especially
from an empathy, people perspective.

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:05.022
And I think business school
is a good place to do that.

00:18:05.022 --> 00:18:09.556
And especially the focus that the GSB
gives on sorta this empathetic leadership

00:18:09.556 --> 00:18:11.324
and leadership development.

00:18:11.324 --> 00:18:13.773
I think is a good thing about that.

00:18:13.773 --> 00:18:18.441
And it just felt right in terms
of I want to do the next step.

00:18:18.441 --> 00:18:23.398
But I never specifically designed
a timeline where I knew it was right.

00:18:23.398 --> 00:18:25.743
So, yeah.

00:18:25.743 --> 00:18:26.684
&gt;&gt; What about for you?

00:18:26.684 --> 00:18:30.051
Because I think for us we had a more
traditional kind of consulting path,

00:18:30.051 --> 00:18:32.767
and it's kind of suggested to you,
but you decided that.

00:18:32.767 --> 00:18:34.300
&gt;&gt; So I was very fed up with my job.

00:18:34.300 --> 00:18:35.669
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:18:35.669 --> 00:18:36.847
&gt;&gt; I learned a ton, and

00:18:36.847 --> 00:18:41.990
I had great colleagues, but it was a very
hierarchical German corporate environment.

00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:45.920
And I was basically told I'm doing
a great job, but in order to take on more

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:48.655
responsibility, I have to do
the same job for another five years.

00:18:48.655 --> 00:18:52.580
Maybe with a different title but
basically the same thing.

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:55.750
And there were a bunch of options on
the table, either changing jobs or

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:56.670
going to business school.

00:18:57.740 --> 00:19:00.150
I decided that I would give it a shot,
Harvard or Stanford,

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:04.750
because I figured that if I spent that
much money, I might as well do it on

00:19:04.750 --> 00:19:08.000
one of those schools that have a huge,
grand recognition.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:13.080
And that worked out well, and
then I figured I would come and do it.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:16.530
So, similar to Dominik,
I hadn't mapped it out.

00:19:16.530 --> 00:19:18.820
Had it not worked,
I might have spent another year at my job.

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:20.840
I might have gone to
a different business school.

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:23.497
But I did not have the five year plan.

00:19:23.497 --> 00:19:26.097
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:19:26.097 --> 00:19:28.506
&gt;&gt; So let's move to another question from

00:19:28.506 --> 00:19:30.390
the audience.

00:19:30.390 --> 00:19:32.440
That's actually an interesting one.

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:37.063
So how has the Stanford experience been
different to what you guys expected?

00:19:37.063 --> 00:19:41.034
Is there anything you did not come see,
besides the fact that there are opera and

00:19:41.034 --> 00:19:42.467
musical stars in the class?

00:19:42.467 --> 00:19:45.584
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I think for me,

00:19:45.584 --> 00:19:50.955
what has surprised me is that how much

00:19:50.955 --> 00:19:55.992
kind of social activity there is.

00:19:55.992 --> 00:20:00.795
So for me, I think I'm an introvert
by nature, and just like coming here,

00:20:00.795 --> 00:20:05.144
and the way, how much there is and
how much there is to experience and

00:20:05.144 --> 00:20:08.371
how much energy that actually
takes was new to me.

00:20:08.371 --> 00:20:12.859
So I've never, I think I've never
experienced as many dinners,

00:20:12.859 --> 00:20:17.263
as many parties, as many every things,
and so it's kind of a very

00:20:17.263 --> 00:20:21.830
intense environment, not only from
a kinda school perspective and

00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:26.507
everything else just going on in
your mind, but also just socially.

00:20:26.507 --> 00:20:29.955
And it's a happy surprise
because it's wonderful.

00:20:29.955 --> 00:20:34.479
But it also takes enough energy and kinda
brain space to think a lot of how all of

00:20:34.479 --> 00:20:37.348
this sorta productivity
kinda comes together.

00:20:37.348 --> 00:20:38.881
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, agree.

00:20:38.881 --> 00:20:40.627
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's definitely,

00:20:40.627 --> 00:20:43.822
what was surprising for me is,
we talked about it before, right,

00:20:43.822 --> 00:20:45.719
that it actually rains in California.

00:20:45.719 --> 00:20:47.912
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They don't tell you that before.

00:20:47.912 --> 00:20:48.492
&gt;&gt; Well, twice a year.

00:20:48.492 --> 00:20:50.992
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:20:50.992 --> 00:20:52.905
those two days have been the last two.

00:20:52.905 --> 00:20:55.010
No, actually it has been fairly good.

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:58.715
I was actually surprised
how cool people are.

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:02.110
Because when you think
about business school,

00:21:02.110 --> 00:21:06.500
you think of that as being one of the
puzzle pieces in your professional life.

00:21:06.500 --> 00:21:10.930
But I think also in the personal life,
it has shaped me a lot because it gives

00:21:10.930 --> 00:21:13.920
you two years to breathe,
take a step back,

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:18.200
look at what you've done, look at who you
are, and look at what you wanna do after.

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:21.440
And I thought it would be a way to
acquire some more tools in order to be

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:22.610
a better manager.

00:21:22.610 --> 00:21:26.330
But I think the introspection,
the level of self-reflection, and

00:21:26.330 --> 00:21:27.460
the time I have for that-
&gt;&gt; That's true.

00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:28.740
&gt;&gt; Was pretty impressive.

00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:34.740
And one class in particular that we have
to basically talk about is touchy feely,

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:36.380
interpersonal dynamics.

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:37.160
Desiree has taken it.

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:37.879
Dominik, not yet.

00:21:37.879 --> 00:21:38.488
&gt;&gt; Not yet.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:21:38.488 --> 00:21:41.114
&gt;&gt; So he does not know what we're talking

00:21:41.114 --> 00:21:41.694
about.

00:21:41.694 --> 00:21:45.061
What it basically is, is,
I think, 12 people or 16 people?

00:21:45.061 --> 00:21:45.897
&gt;&gt; Yeah, 12.

00:21:45.897 --> 00:21:51.810
&gt;&gt; 12 people in a room without
instructions, a script, anything.

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:56.260
Two facilitators but basically 12
people sitting next to each other and

00:21:56.260 --> 00:21:57.950
having nothing to talk about.

00:21:57.950 --> 00:22:00.390
And then you start talking about things.

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:04.370
For example, I might ask, Desiree,
hey, should we talk about norms and

00:22:04.370 --> 00:22:06.618
when to arrive for class?

00:22:06.618 --> 00:22:10.470
And then Desiree might tell me, well,
Tim, by you asking that question,

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:13.920
you implicitly took on a leadership
role in this class and-

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:15.705
&gt;&gt; Which I'm not comfortable with.

00:22:15.705 --> 00:22:16.541
&gt;&gt; I don't feel comfortable with that.

00:22:16.541 --> 00:22:19.493
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And you get feedback on your actions,

00:22:19.493 --> 00:22:23.518
live, and in way you usually don't
get it in a work environment.

00:22:23.518 --> 00:22:26.308
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that actually speaks to one
of the questions around kinda how to

00:22:26.308 --> 00:22:29.397
develop your soft skills and know more
about your strengths and weaknesses.

00:22:29.397 --> 00:22:32.172
And it's phenomenal.

00:22:32.172 --> 00:22:33.315
So I'm just in the last-
&gt;&gt; You

00:22:33.315 --> 00:22:33.855
said you fell in love with the class.

00:22:33.855 --> 00:22:36.200
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:39.337
&gt;&gt; I have my last class tomorrow where we

00:22:39.337 --> 00:22:42.170
share the final feedback which is

00:22:42.170 --> 00:22:47.740
basically five hours of feedback
to 12 people in a room.

00:22:47.740 --> 00:22:50.900
And its only one kind
of small piece of it.

00:22:50.900 --> 00:22:55.230
in general, we spend a lot of time during
night class sitting around together and

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:58.700
just learning about each other, learning
what actually works, what doesn't,

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:00.515
what works for you,
how do you react to things.

00:23:00.515 --> 00:23:04.620
You just get a lot more self-aware and
know what works for you, but

00:23:04.620 --> 00:23:08.440
you also learn a lot about how other
people interact and kind of trying to

00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:12.310
hone your skills of how to actually
work in groups and how that works.

00:23:12.310 --> 00:23:16.280
So I thought it was a phenomenal
experience when it comes to soft skills.

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:18.790
But there are also a lot of others,
so maybe you want to do leadership?

00:23:18.790 --> 00:23:23.510
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I think in general,
even beyond that, that's just one element.

00:23:23.510 --> 00:23:25.600
But what was surprising for

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:28.980
me is how deliberately the GSB
actually shapes culture, right?

00:23:28.980 --> 00:23:33.615
And you spend a lot of time talking about
norms and how people interact together,

00:23:33.615 --> 00:23:37.501
and all of those different sessions and
courses come together, and

00:23:37.501 --> 00:23:39.298
you sort of develop a culture.

00:23:39.298 --> 00:23:43.990
And for me it was really surprising to
see that it's almost like you engineer

00:23:43.990 --> 00:23:49.213
a certain culture, right, and this
openness, vulnerability, feedback, etc.

00:23:49.213 --> 00:23:49.979
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:49.979 --> 00:23:52.975
It feels almost like a DNA of the GSB.

00:23:52.975 --> 00:23:57.962
And we've been though the first year, and
then now it seems like, almost, we sort

00:23:57.962 --> 00:24:02.970
of, got that DNA, and feel comfortable
with it, and sort of live it ourselves.

00:24:02.970 --> 00:24:04.976
And I think that's, yeah.

00:24:04.976 --> 00:24:08.800
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I was almost worried to go
back to the real world and experience,

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:14.360
everybody's not as vulnerable, and open,
and sharing, and caring as we are here.

00:24:14.360 --> 00:24:18.170
&gt;&gt; No I think it's a very
special environment here.

00:24:18.170 --> 00:24:20.930
And there's certain things you can
take back into the real world,

00:24:20.930 --> 00:24:24.100
and some things you might want to keep for
yourself.

00:24:24.100 --> 00:24:26.190
Talking about the real world.

00:24:26.190 --> 00:24:28.430
What are you guys going
to do over the summer?

00:24:28.430 --> 00:24:34.430
Maybe you could talk about the recruiting
process for summer internships.

00:24:34.430 --> 00:24:36.651
Where are you guys going to
head out in like two weeks.

00:24:39.784 --> 00:24:40.350
[INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:40.350 --> 00:24:42.540
&gt;&gt; I'm just going to stay here.

00:24:42.540 --> 00:24:46.210
&gt;&gt; I'm going to stay here so I cannot
talk to much about recruiting process.

00:24:47.370 --> 00:24:50.360
Maybe you can add a little bit after that.

00:24:50.360 --> 00:24:55.340
But yeah we're using the summer in this
very specific three month period to

00:24:55.340 --> 00:24:59.060
actually start a business
in insurance space.

00:24:59.060 --> 00:25:00.620
So we're trying to use technology and

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:02.930
insurance, and
build something around that.

00:25:02.930 --> 00:25:04.230
And, yeah.

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:10.015
I think it sort of felt like the first
year, for me, was taking a step back.

00:25:10.015 --> 00:25:11.190
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:25:11.190 --> 00:25:12.670
&gt;&gt; Learning a lot of new things.

00:25:12.670 --> 00:25:16.060
But also building out a team to do that,
right?

00:25:16.060 --> 00:25:19.500
And so one of the team members,
actually, from the GSB.

00:25:20.740 --> 00:25:25.780
Right now, it's on this whole
ramping up period for that.

00:25:25.780 --> 00:25:29.720
So instead of recruiting,
we did a business plan and

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:32.320
what service you can
probably substitute that.

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:32.980
For the summer,

00:25:32.980 --> 00:25:37.920
I'm actually staying here in Paulo Alto
also, and we're starting a business here.

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:41.530
&gt;&gt; So I maybe took a more
traditional recruiting path.

00:25:41.530 --> 00:25:44.620
There are a bunch of on
campus recruiting events.

00:25:44.620 --> 00:25:47.860
Companies coming to campus, and
talking about their recruiting process and

00:25:47.860 --> 00:25:49.780
then starting that process.

00:25:49.780 --> 00:25:53.780
Mainly big corporates from the US,
some from Europe, consulting companies,

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:54.670
finance companies.

00:25:55.960 --> 00:25:58.060
That is the on campus recruiting.

00:25:58.060 --> 00:26:02.880
Then there is it's like
a smaller SNP recruiting.

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:06.090
One of the key events at the GSB
is the fewer than 300 event for

00:26:06.090 --> 00:26:09.350
companies that have less than
300 employees come to campus.

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:13.240
And it's very informal casual setting,
and you get to talk to up and coming

00:26:13.240 --> 00:26:17.240
companies, even PRIDE, and those kind of
companies, usually tech in the valley.

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:19.785
And then there is networking
based recruiting.

00:26:19.785 --> 00:26:24.930
So you can meet with
Career Management employees,

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:29.300
and they help you kind of find people that
work in the space that you wanna be in,

00:26:29.300 --> 00:26:30.940
and can connect you directly.

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:36.470
Given that the class size is fairly small
with 400, the community is very strong.

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:40.140
So even if people 20 years
before you graduate,

00:26:40.140 --> 00:26:41.430
are now in an interesting position.

00:26:41.430 --> 00:26:47.870
They will usually return your email, or
your call, and get back back to you.

00:26:47.870 --> 00:26:50.740
I see we are almost running out of time,
that went by quickly.

00:26:50.740 --> 00:26:55.030
With four more minutes to go,
before we go, Dominic, Desiree,

00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:59.790
any words of final advice for the people
that are thinking about applying

00:26:59.790 --> 00:27:03.631
to Standford, and
your GSB highlight in year number one?

00:27:07.647 --> 00:27:09.720
Ladies first I guess.

00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:19.190
&gt;&gt; In terms of advice,

00:27:19.190 --> 00:27:26.640
I would say fight through the application
process, and the GMAT, and all that.

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:29.006
Is a lot of work and
it's kind of stressful.

00:27:29.006 --> 00:27:30.207
And especially for me,

00:27:30.207 --> 00:27:33.278
it was a stressful period doing
that kind of next to your job.

00:27:33.278 --> 00:27:35.483
But it's incredibly worth it.

00:27:35.483 --> 00:27:40.032
And this time here has been one of
the most amazing of my life and so

00:27:40.032 --> 00:27:42.290
I'm incredibly.

00:27:42.290 --> 00:27:45.080
Thankful and I just want to
tell you that it's going to be,

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:49.840
coming out of this whole process you're
gonna be here and it's wonderful.

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:53.480
What's the most amazing moment for me?

00:27:53.480 --> 00:27:56.210
I think there have been a few ones.

00:27:56.210 --> 00:27:58.342
&gt;&gt; You can have only one.

00:27:58.342 --> 00:28:01.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:28:01.950 --> 00:28:02.890
Maybe you can think of one.

00:28:02.890 --> 00:28:03.620
&gt;&gt; I'll think about it.

00:28:03.620 --> 00:28:05.860
&gt;&gt; Maybe you'll come up with one, perhaps.

00:28:05.860 --> 00:28:07.230
Dominic?
&gt;&gt; Yeah, same here.

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:12.724
I think just do it is
probably a good way to, yeah.

00:28:12.724 --> 00:28:15.590
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:15.590 --> 00:28:19.810
I think, people tell you that the
application process itself is valuable.

00:28:21.050 --> 00:28:24.930
For me, back then it was sort of,
I couldn't believe that, but actually is,

00:28:24.930 --> 00:28:26.400
in a certain way.

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:28.450
Cuz it forces you to reflect yourself.

00:28:28.450 --> 00:28:33.520
Forces you to engage also the people you
ask for a recommendation on a very, sort

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:39.600
of, deeper feedback conversation, and,
sort of, get all of those things together.

00:28:39.600 --> 00:28:45.100
So I think just do it,
is probably the right thing for me to say.

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:48.580
And when it comes to a favorite moment,
it's hard to pinpoint.

00:28:48.580 --> 00:28:51.100
Cuz there's actually so much happening.

00:28:51.100 --> 00:28:55.910
It feels like one day of GSB is
probably like a week of normal life.

00:28:55.910 --> 00:28:59.325
&gt;&gt; It's like with dogs, one year's like
two year, three human lives or something.

00:28:59.325 --> 00:29:00.560
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:29:00.560 --> 00:29:04.010
But I think what was really
cool experience in general is

00:29:04.010 --> 00:29:05.630
stuff with the GST what we do here.

00:29:05.630 --> 00:29:08.460
So we have to do this, global study trips.

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:11.118
I took the global route
to the US actually.

00:29:11.118 --> 00:29:14.990
[LAUGH] But it's sort of this
study trip where you actually

00:29:14.990 --> 00:29:17.460
get to know a country deeper and better.

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:20.880
And you're in a group of 20,
30 people roughly.

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:25.090
You have a lot of meetings
with professionals,

00:29:25.090 --> 00:29:28.550
with politicians, industry leaders
together, sort of the culture.

00:29:28.550 --> 00:29:30.970
But you also get to know
your peers really well.

00:29:30.970 --> 00:29:35.100
I think this GST experience for
me was pretty cool.

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:36.272
I mean we had phenomenal meetings.

00:29:36.272 --> 00:29:40.320
We've been to New York, for example,
where we met Micheal Bloomberg.

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:43.630
And you have a pretty intense and

00:29:43.630 --> 00:29:47.070
open discussion with those people and
for me this was cool.

00:29:47.070 --> 00:29:48.870
&gt;&gt; Mm hm.
&gt;&gt; it was a pretty cool experience.

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:51.470
&gt;&gt; My piece of advice be yourself.

00:29:51.470 --> 00:29:55.513
Really show case what makes you different,
and don't talk about you know,

00:29:55.513 --> 00:29:59.321
what makes you similar to a lot of
people that might have gone to the GSP.

00:29:59.321 --> 00:30:03.780
The class aims to be as
diverse as possible.

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:07.110
So whatever makes you special should
definitely be in the application.

00:30:07.110 --> 00:30:12.230
And my highlight was, in my first year,
I had been working in Rwanda.

00:30:12.230 --> 00:30:15.190
And I had the change to have lunch with
Paul Kagame, the President of Rwanda.

00:30:15.190 --> 00:30:17.460
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; Which was really interesting.

00:30:17.460 --> 00:30:20.930
Getting to speak to a person
like that one on one.

00:30:20.930 --> 00:30:23.660
And I think that's basically it.

00:30:23.660 --> 00:30:28.050
Thank you so much Desiree and
Dominic for joining us this morning, and

00:30:28.050 --> 00:30:30.220
thanks to all of you for participating.

00:30:30.220 --> 00:30:34.820
If you'd like to hear more about the GSB,
you can meet an MBA admissions officer in

00:30:34.820 --> 00:30:37.960
person while they travel to many
cities throughout Europe this summer.

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:39.040
I believe Germany, Austria and

00:30:39.040 --> 00:30:41.690
Switzerland, are probably
amongst their route.

00:30:41.690 --> 00:30:44.120
Please keep an eye out for
the invitations to meet them, or

00:30:44.120 --> 00:30:47.140
check the events page on
the Stanford MBA website.

00:30:47.140 --> 00:30:50.160
You can also learn more about the MBA
program, admission requirements, and

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:52.090
financial aid on the website.

00:30:52.090 --> 00:30:56.910
Application for the class starting in the
fall of 2016 will be available in June.

00:30:56.910 --> 00:31:01.141
Finally, please take a moment to answer a
